  en: `
<h1>When the Machine Looks Back</h1>
<p class="lead">A modern language model opens a space of comparison far beyond the reach of a single human lifetime.</p>
<p>The order of magnitude can be estimated roughly. The data centres behind current frontier models provide raw computational capacity on the scale of roughly ten to one hundred tightly interconnected human brains. This scale describes computational substrate; judgement is a different category. An institutional comparison is therefore more useful than comparison with a single exceptional individual: in computational substrate, a frontier model is <strong>far more like</strong> a Bletchley Park, a Los Alamos, or an early NASA under von Braun — a densely networked, coordinated collective of specialists.</p>
<p>The knowledge substrate is similarly concrete. A human brain processes a few thousand books over a lifetime — fragmentarily, selectively, typically in three or four languages. A frontier model has been trained on text <strong>equivalent to hundreds of millions of books</strong>: across dozens of languages, centuries and disciplines.</p>
<p>It also invents sources, hallucinates and can be confidently wrong. Judgement therefore has to be considered separately from raw capacity. When it places very different situations next to one another — one failed forecast beside another, an institutional warning chain beside a technical defect, a well-founded decision with a bad outcome beside a poorly founded one with a good outcome — a peculiar viewpoint emerges.</p>
<p class="punch">A foreign viewpoint.<br><strong>Foreign enough to be useful.</strong></p>
<h2>The outside view</h2>
<p>A human watching a marmoset solve a task may immediately see why the animal keeps searching in the same direction even though the solution lies elsewhere. From inside the task, that structure remains hidden to the animal. The decisive feature is the viewpoint: it is looking from within the task.</p>
<p>With us, this is harder. We are inside our decisions. We know their history, our loyalties, our justifications. And above all, we usually know the outcome — which turns the path leading there into an almost inevitable story in retrospect. It almost never was.</p>
<p>A language model can hold very different situations side by side and thereby make that distortion observable. The app turns this foreign viewpoint into a tool for decisions, evidence and revision.</p>
<h2>Two parts</h2>
<p>In the first, you enter decision situations yourself. You receive information in the order in which it becomes available. You choose what you want to know next. You form hypotheses, defend or revise them — and only later learn what followed.</p>
<p>In the second, the same questions are put to real, documented cases: Germany's Federal Network Agency during the 2022 gas crisis; Bundesbank inflation projections confronted by a rapidly changing inflation environment; Siemens Gamesa, where technical reality collided with corporate planning; Munich Re, which insures risks for which historical experience is often scarce; and ERGO's 2011 Riester case, which shows how a serious investigation can still ask the diagnostically wrong question.</p>
<p>In these cases we know the outcome. The focus is the path: What information was available at the time? What was sought? What changed the judgement — and which evidence had genuine diagnostic power?</p>
<h2>Six movements</h2>
<p>Across both parts, the app observes six ways of dealing with a resistant reality. They are called <strong>H, K, Q, D, G</strong> and <strong>R</strong> and together form a multidimensional “CT scan” of the movement of thought in a particular situation.</p>
<p><strong>H</strong> is noticing when one's model no longer fits. Many consequential failures begin one step before the visible answer: an anomaly is dismissed as an exception, a silent assumption remains untested, a question disappears from the search space.</p>
<p><strong>K</strong> is the contact between model and outside world. A system can be intellectually sophisticated and increasingly talk only to itself. Bad news does not reach the decisive level; measurements contradict a strategy but the strategy is stronger than the measurement. K asks whether external signals can actually change the model.</p>
<p><strong>Q</strong> is the discriminating power of the information being sought. What matters is the discriminating power of the information. Large amounts of data can still miss the relevant error class when it is absent from the chosen sample. Q asks which information actually separates competing explanations.</p>
<p><strong>D</strong> is the search for the mechanism beneath the event. A turbine fails; a forecast misses; a sales organisation develops problematic patterns. D focuses on the mechanisms and conditions that made the result possible or more likely.</p>
<p><strong>G</strong> is the generation of new possibilities: another hypothesis, an unexpected comparison, a question that had not been asked before. Generativity gains epistemic value through its interaction with world coupling, horizon awareness and revision.</p>
<p><strong>R</strong> asks what happens when new evidence arrives. Sometimes judgement changes too quickly for weak reasons, sometimes barely at all despite strong reasons. The point is whether the degree of revision is proportionate to the strength of the evidence — in either direction.</p>
<p>These six movements interact, amplify one another, compensate for one another or produce characteristic tensions.</p>
<h2>What makes the app different</h2>
<p>Every case forces a separation that everyday judgement routinely collapses.</p>
<ol class="three-levels"><li><strong>How did the reasoning process unfold?</strong></li><li><strong>Was the decision well-founded given what was known at the time?</strong></li><li><strong>What actually happened?</strong></li></ol>
<p>A well-founded decision can turn out badly. A poorly founded one can succeed by luck. A careful forecasting method can produce a large individual error; a poor method can hit by chance. Judging only outcomes can teach exactly the wrong lesson.</p>
<p>That is why many situations initially hide the outcome. You first receive only the information that was available at the time — and only afterwards learn what happened. The sequence counters hindsight bias: once we know the ending, the path towards it looks far more inevitable than it was.</p>
<h2>The second lens</h2>
<p>In the fictional self-experiment, another perspective is added.</p>
<p>Italian economist Carlo M. Cipolla proposed a radically simple consequence model in a satirical essay. It centres on two axes: what the actor gains or loses, and what others gain or lose. The four resulting fields carry deliberately provocative labels. In this app, the model serves as a satirical consequence lens for fictional situations.</p>
<p>It puts two different questions side by side: How was the decision produced — and what did the action cause?</p>
<p class="punch"><strong>That is the Dual Lens.</strong></p>
<p>For real people, companies and public bodies, the app switches to source-grounded episode reconstruction using H/K/Q/D/G/R. It reconstructs the state of knowledge at the time, the decision, new evidence, the ex-post outcome, counter-readings and the scope of robust conclusions.</p>
<h2>What becomes visible</h2>
<p>Many decisions — in companies, public bodies, forecasts and everyday life — contain the same difficult moment: the model is no longer sufficient. A signal contradicts expectations. Something does not fit.</p>
<p>Then something is decided. Is the signal integrated? Ignored? Rewritten into the old story? Does it lead to the right next question — or the wrong one?</p>
<p>This app shows what that moment looks like: in constructed situations in which you decide, and in documented real cases whose outcomes are now known.</p>
`
